/* Original classroom drafts. Teacher review and full syllabus audit pending. */
const chapters = [
{id:'start',week:1,title:'Your first Python program',intro:'A program transforms inputs into useful outputs. Start with a small problem you can explain, then translate your steps into Python.',objectives:['Identify inputs, processing and outputs.','Run and edit a Python script.','Trace a sequence and compare expected with actual output.'],sections:[
['A problem before a program',"<p>A school club sells badges for $3 each. To work out the cost, the program follows three steps:</p><ol><li>Read the number of badges. This is the <strong>input</strong>.</li><li>Multiply that number by 3. This is the <strong>processing</strong>.</li><li>Display the cost. This is the <strong>output</strong>.</li></ol><p>These ordered steps form an <strong>algorithm</strong>: a method for solving a problem.</p><p>For now, assume the user enters zero or a positive whole number. We will check for unsuitable input in week 7. Write down what the program must do before coding. This statement is a <strong>requirement</strong>. For example: “Display the cost for any valid badge quantity.” You can test whether the program meets it.</p>"],
['Prepare your workspace',"<p>Open your school’s approved Python 3 editor or IDE. An <strong>IDE</strong> brings tools for writing and running code together. Then:</p><ol><li>Create a folder for your work.</li><li>Save the example as <code>badge_cost.py</code>.</li><li>Run it using the editor’s Run command. If your terminal is set up for Python, you can use <code>python badge_cost.py</code> instead.</li><li>Enter the number of badges when the program asks.</li></ol><p>Save each task in its own file and give it a name that describes its purpose. Save your changes before running. Avoid names such as <code>random.py</code>: Python already has a standard module with that name, and your file could be loaded instead.</p>"],
['Sequence: follow the order',"<p>Read the program from top to bottom. In <code>total = quantity * BADGE_PRICE</code>, Python works out the multiplication first. It then makes the name <code>total</code> refer to the result. This is <strong>assignment</strong>.</p><p><code>input</code> reads text. <code>int</code> converts suitable text to an integer so you can calculate with it. The name <code>BADGE_PRICE</code> shows what the value 3 means. We intend this value to stay the same, so we use it as a <strong>constant</strong>. For four badges, predict <code>Total: $12</code>. Check the result against your prediction: running without an error does not prove that a program gives the right answer.</p>"],
['Trace and test',"<table><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Expected cost</th><th>Reason</th></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>$0</td><td>No badges</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>$3</td><td>One item</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>$12</td><td>Typical order</td></tr></table><p>Calculate the expected cost yourself before running each test. Write the program’s actual result beside it. If they differ, work through the instructions to find where the result first goes wrong.</p><p>A <strong>syntax error</strong> means Python cannot parse the instructions. A <strong>logic error</strong> means the instructions give the wrong result, even though the program may run.</p>"]
],code:'BADGE_PRICE = 3\nquantity = int(input("Number of badges: "))\ntotal = quantity * BADGE_PRICE\nprint(f"Total: ${total}")\n',practice:[['Predict','What happens when the quantity is 6?','The total is 18, so the output is Total: $18.'],['Modify','Add a fixed $2 packaging charge. Test quantities 0 and 3.','Use total = quantity * BADGE_PRICE + 2. Results are $2 and $11. Clarify whether packaging applies to a zero-item order; requirements determine that behaviour.'],['Explain','Why should you write an expected result before running the program?','An independent expected result lets you check correctness instead of accepting whatever the program prints.']],task:"Make a book-cover calculator. Each cover costs $2, and every order has a $4 handling charge. State which inputs you assume are allowed. Write the input, calculation and output steps, then code them. Record three tests and compare the results with your predictions.",solution:'COVER_PRICE = 2\nHANDLING = 4\nquantity = int(input("Covers: "))\ntotal = quantity * COVER_PRICE + HANDLING\nprint(f"Total: ${total}")\n# For quantities 0, 1, 5 expect 4, 6, 14.\n',recap:'Define the requirement, write the steps, implement a sequence, and compare actual results with independently calculated expectations.',lessons:['Diagnose prior knowledge and decompose the badge problem.','Set up the editor and run the example.','Trace, modify and test a sequential program.','Complete the book-cover task and explain the results.'],reference:'https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html',refTitle:'Python built-in functions',quiz:{q:'Which step is processing in the badge program?',options:['Read the quantity','Multiply quantity by price','Display the total'],answer:1,explanation:'Processing transforms the input into the required result.'}},
{id:'values',week:2,title:'Values, types and operators',intro:'The same-looking input can mean different things. Choose a type that matches the meaning of the data and the operations you need.',objectives:['Choose integer, float, string and Boolean types.','Convert input and use arithmetic operators.','Explain assignment and operator precedence.'],sections:[
['Match the type to the meaning',"<table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Example</th><th>Use</th></tr><tr><td>int</td><td>24</td><td>Count of items</td></tr><tr><td>float</td><td>2.5</td><td>Measured quantity</td></tr><tr><td>str</td><td>\"0042\"</td><td>Identifier with leading zeros</td></tr><tr><td>bool</td><td>True</td><td>Two-state decision</td></tr></table><p>Ask what a value represents before choosing its type. A postcode such as “0042” is an identifier, not a quantity. Keep it as text so the leading zeros remain. Converting it to an integer gives 42.</p>"],
['Convert deliberately',"<p>If you type 12 into <code>input()</code>, Python receives the text <code>\"12\"</code>, not the number 12. Adding text joins it together: <code>\"12\" + \"3\"</code> gives <code>\"123\"</code>. Convert numeric text before doing arithmetic.</p><p>Choose a conversion that matches the input:</p><ul><li><code>int(\"12\")</code> gives the integer 12.</li><li><code>int(\"12.5\")</code> raises <code>ValueError</code> because the text is not an integer.</li><li><code>float(\"12.5\")</code> gives 12.5.</li></ul><p>Later, you will learn to check input and handle conversion errors. A finished application must deal with unsuitable input.</p>"],
['Calculate and predict',"<p>Suppose you pack 17 badges into bags that hold 4 each. You can fill 4 bags and have 1 badge left. In Python, <code>17 // 4</code> gives 4 and <code>17 % 4</code> gives 1. The <code>%</code> operator finds the <strong>remainder</strong>.</p><p>Use <code>+</code>, <code>-</code>, <code>*</code> and <code>/</code> to add, subtract, multiply and divide. With the integers and floats used here, <code>/</code> gives a float. The <code>//</code> operator rounds the division result down. For non-negative integers, it tells you how many full groups fit; negative results need extra care. Python multiplies and divides before it adds. For example, <code>2 + 3 * 4</code> is 14. Parentheses change the order: <code>(2 + 3) * 4</code> is 20.</p>"],
['Assignment updates a value',"<p>If <code>count</code> is 4, <code>count = count + 1</code> changes it to 5. Read the instruction as “work out the old count plus one, then store the result using the name count”. The <code>=</code> sign assigns a value. Use <code>==</code> when you want to compare values.</p><p>Some decimal fractions cannot be stored exactly as binary floating-point values. Showing two decimal places only changes the displayed text; it does not make the stored value exact. These examples are for learning. For financial software, choose a suitable representation, such as integer cents or decimal arithmetic.</p>"]
],code:'badges = 17\nBAG_SIZE = 4\nfull_bags = badges // BAG_SIZE\nleft_over = badges % BAG_SIZE\nprint(f"Full bags: {full_bags}")\nprint(f"Remaining badges: {left_over}")\n',practice:[['Predict','What are 23 // 5 and 23 % 5?','4 and 3: four complete groups of five account for 20 items, leaving three.'],['Choose','Which type would you use for a postcode and why?','A string. It is an identifier; leading zeros may matter and arithmetic is not meaningful.'],['Debug','Why does input("Age: ") + 1 fail?','The input is a string and 1 is an integer. Convert valid input with int before arithmetic, and handle invalid input in a robust application.']],task:"Pack 29 cones into boxes that hold six cones each. Display how many full boxes you can fill and how many cones remain. Before changing the code, predict the results for 0, 6 and 7 cones.",solution:'cones = 29\nBOX_SIZE = 6\nprint(cones // BOX_SIZE)\nprint(cones % BOX_SIZE)\n# 29 -> 4 and 5; 0 -> 0 and 0; 6 -> 1 and 0; 7 -> 1 and 1.\n',recap:"Choose a data type that fits the value’s meaning. Convert text before arithmetic, use = to assign and == to compare, and check calculations by hand.",lessons:['Compare types and convert input.','Practice operators, precedence and trace updates.','Work on representation in the next chapter.','Complete conversion and representation practice.'],reference:'https://docs.python.org/3/library/stdtypes.html',refTitle:'Python standard types',quiz:{q:'What does "4" + "5" produce?',options:['9','"45"','A Boolean'],answer:1,explanation:'Both operands are strings, so + concatenates them.'}},
{id:'representation',week:2,title:'Numbers and characters in a computer',intro:'Binary, decimal and hexadecimal are different ways to write a number. Character encodings connect numbers with text.',objectives:['Convert small non-negative integers between bases.','Explain hexadecimal digits and place values.','Distinguish a character, a code point and encoded bytes.'],sections:[
['Place value changes with the base',"<p>In decimal, 247 means <code>2 × 100 + 4 × 10 + 7 × 1</code>. Binary works the same way, but each position is worth twice the position to its right. It uses only 0 and 1. For binary 10110, add the positions containing 1: <code>16 + 4 + 2 = 22</code>.</p><table><tr><th>Place</th><td>16</td><td>8</td><td>4</td><td>2</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><th>Digit</th><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td></tr></table><p>To write decimal 22 in binary, choose 16, 4 and 2 from the place values. Put 1 in those positions and 0 in the others. Adding zeros at the front does not change the value: 00010110 is still 22.</p>"],
['Hexadecimal is a compact notation',"<p>Hexadecimal uses 16 digits: 0–9, then A–F for values 10–15. For example, 2F means <code>2 × 16 + 15 = 47</code>. Each hexadecimal digit matches four binary digits. The 2 becomes 0010 and the F becomes 1111, so 2F becomes 00101111.</p><p>In Python, <code>0b</code> marks a binary number and <code>0x</code> marks a hexadecimal number. These prefixes show how to read the digits. A <strong>bit</strong> is one binary digit. Eight bits make one <strong>byte</strong>.</p>"],
['Text needs an agreed representation',"<p>Computers also use numbers to represent text. Unicode assigns the character “A” the number 65. This number is its <strong>code point</strong>, written <code>U+0041</code> in hexadecimal. Decimal 65, binary 1000001 and hexadecimal 41 all represent the same code-point number.</p><p>A code point identifies a character; it does not tell you how many bytes a file uses for it. A <strong>character encoding</strong>, such as UTF-8, gives the rules for turning code points into bytes. “A” uses one byte in UTF-8, but many other characters need more. A visible character is not always eight bits.</p>"],
['Check by a second method',"<p>Work out a conversion by hand first. Then use Python to check it:</p><ul><li><code>bin</code> writes an integer in binary.</li><li><code>hex</code> writes an integer in hexadecimal.</li><li><code>int(text, base)</code> reads digits in the base you specify.</li><li><code>ord</code> gives a single character’s code point; <code>chr</code> turns the code point back into a character.</li></ul><p>The example shows the code point for “A” in three bases. The digits change, but the value stays the same. Convert back to check that you still have the original number.</p>"]
],code:'character = "A"\ncode_point = ord(character)\nprint(code_point)\nprint(bin(code_point))\nprint(hex(code_point))\nprint(chr(code_point))\n# Expected: 65, 0b1000001, 0x41, A\n',practice:[['Convert','Convert binary 11001 to decimal.','16 + 8 + 1 = 25.'],['Convert','Convert hexadecimal 3C to decimal and eight-bit binary.','3×16 + 12 = 60. Binary is 00111100.'],['Explain','Does Unicode code point 233 mean a UTF-8 file stores the character in one byte?','No. A code point identifies a character; UTF-8 defines its byte encoding. The character é, U+00E9, uses two UTF-8 bytes.']],task:"Convert decimal 37 to binary and hexadecimal by hand. Check both answers with Python. Convert each answer back to decimal to check that you still have 37.",solution:'number = 37\nprint(bin(number))  # 0b100101\nprint(hex(number))  # 0x25\nprint(int("100101", 2))  # 37\nprint(int("25", 16))  # 37\n',recap:'Base changes the notation, not the value. Character encoding connects text to numeric representations, and byte storage depends on the encoding.',lessons:['Shared week 2: types and arithmetic first.','Use place values to convert numbers.','Group binary into four-bit hexadecimal digits.','Explain code points and check conversions.'],reference:'https://docs.python.org/3/howto/unicode.html',refTitle:'Python Unicode HOWTO',quiz:{q:'What decimal value does hexadecimal 1A represent?',options:['10','26','110'],answer:1,explanation:'1×16 + 10 = 26.'}},
{id:'loops',week:4,title:'Repeating work with loops',intro:'A loop repeats instructions. This repetition is called iteration. Decide what repeats, what changes, and what makes the loop stop.',objectives:['Choose a for or while loop for a task.','Use counters and accumulators.','Trace a loop and test its stopping condition.'],sections:[
['A fixed number of repetitions',"<p>A club records three donations. Each time, the program reads an amount and adds it to a running total. Set the total to zero before the loop. Read and add inside the loop, then display the final total after the loop.</p><p>The <strong>loop body</strong> is the indented code that repeats. Keep the final display outside that indentation so it runs once, after all three donations.</p><p><code>range(3)</code> gives 0, 1 and 2. <code>range(1, 4)</code> gives 1, 2 and 3. In both cases, the stop value is left out. A <strong>counter</strong> counts how often something happens. An <strong>accumulator</strong> combines values, such as adding donations to a total.</p>"],
['Trace the running total',"<table><tr><th>Iteration</th><th>Donation</th><th>Total before</th><th>Total after</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>5</td><td>0</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>7</td><td>5</td><td>12</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>3</td><td>12</td><td>15</td></tr></table><p>Follow the total through each row. It changes from 0 to 5, then 12, then 15. This record of changing values is a <strong>trace table</strong>. If you reset the total to zero inside the loop, you lose the earlier donations.</p>"],
['Repeat while a condition remains true',"<p>Use a <code>while</code> loop when a condition decides whether to repeat. Start with <code>count = 1</code>. While <code>count &lt;= 3</code>, print the count and add 1. Python checks the condition before each pass, so the body might not run at all.</p><p>Make sure something can eventually stop the loop. In this example, increasing <code>count</code> makes it reach 4, when the condition becomes false. Trace the last pass and the check that stops the loop. Test cases with zero, one and several passes where the task allows them.</p><div class=\"learning-block\"><p class=\"learning-label\">Worked walkthrough</p><h3>Include the check that stops the loop</h3><p>For count = 1 and while count &lt;= 3, check the condition before every pass.</p><figure class=\"learning-visual\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">count before check</th><th scope=\"col\">count &lt;= 3</th><th scope=\"col\">What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>True</td><td>Print 1; change count to 2</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>True</td><td>Print 2; change count to 3</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>True</td><td>Print 3; change count to 4</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>False</td><td>Stop; do not print 4</td></tr></tbody></table><figcaption>The last condition check is not another pass through the body.</figcaption></figure><div class=\"learning-recall\"><p><strong>Pause and predict:</strong> What prints if count starts at 4?</p><details class=\"print-answer-only\"><summary>Show the explanation</summary><p>Nothing. The first condition check is false, so the body runs zero times.</p></details><p><strong>Try a small change:</strong> Change &lt;= to &lt;. Predict the output before tracing it.</p></div></div>"],
['From algorithm to implementation',"<pre>total = 0\nFOR donation_number = 1 TO 3\n    INPUT donation\n    total = total + donation\nEND FOR\nOUTPUT total</pre><p>In this pseudocode, the FOR loop includes both end values: 1, 2 and 3. Python’s <code>range</code> leaves out its stop value. When you explain a loop, show its starting values (<strong>initialisation</strong>), what repeats, and what makes it stop (<strong>termination</strong>).</p><p><strong>Prepare for the score task:</strong> the <strong>mean</strong> is the total divided by the number of values. Read and add four scores inside the loop. After the loop, calculate <code>mean = total / 4</code>. For 8, 10, 6 and 12, the total is 36 and the mean is 9. This task always reads four values; a task allowing no values would need a separate empty-input rule before division.</p>"]
],code:'total = 0\nfor donation_number in range(1, 4):\n    donation = int(input(f"Donation {donation_number}: "))\n    total = total + donation\nprint(f"Total donations: ${total}")\n',practice:[['Predict','Which values appear in range(2, 6)?','2, 3, 4 and 5. The stop value 6 is excluded.'],['Debug','A total contains only the last donation. What should you inspect?','Check whether total = 0 occurs inside the loop. Initialise the accumulator once before repetition.'],['Trace','count starts at 1; while count < 3 prints count then increases it. What prints?','1 and 2. After the second update count is 3, so the condition becomes false.']],task:"Read four whole-number scores and display their total and mean. Assume the input is valid. Predict the results for 8, 10, 6 and 12 before running your code.",solution:'total = 0\nfor score_number in range(4):\n    score = int(input("Score: "))\n    total += score\nmean = total / 4\nprint(total, mean)\n# Expected total 36, mean 9.0.\n',recap:'Initialise before repetition, update the changing state inside it, and test the point where repetition stops.',lessons:['Model fixed repetition with for and range.','Introduce condition-controlled while loops.','Trace counters/accumulators and repair mistakes.','Complete and test the score task.'],reference:'https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/controlflow.html',refTitle:'Python control flow',quiz:{q:'Where should a running total normally be initialised?',options:['Inside every iteration','Once before the loop','After the result is printed'],answer:1,explanation:'Initialising once preserves accumulated values across iterations.'}},
{id:'functions',week:5,title:'Functions and scope',intro:'Give one useful task a name, define its inputs and return its result. Small functions make a program easier to understand and test.',objectives:['Define and call a function.','Distinguish parameters from arguments.','Use return values and explain local/global scope.'],sections:[
['One task, one function',"<p>A <strong>function definition</strong> starts with <code>def</code>, gives the function a name and lists its parameters in round brackets. Its indented body describes what to do. Defining it does not run that body. A <strong>call</strong>, such as <code>badge_cost(4, 3)</code>, runs it with the supplied values.</p><p>Suppose several parts of a program need to work out badge costs. Put that calculation in one function. Give it a quantity and a unit price, and have it return their product. Keep input prompts and receipt printing outside this function.</p><p>These agreed inputs and outputs form the function’s <strong>contract</strong>. In <code>badge_cost(4, 3)</code>, 4 and 3 are the <strong>arguments</strong>: the values you supply. Inside the function, the names <code>quantity</code> and <code>unit_price</code> are <strong>parameters</strong>. They receive those values for the call.</p>"],
['Return a result',"<p>Use <code>return</code> to send a result back to the code that called the function. It also ends that call. The caller can save the result, display it or use it in another calculation.</p><p><code>print</code> only displays output; printing a result does not return it. If a function finishes normally without returning an explicit value, it returns <code>None</code>. If another calculation needs a number, make sure your function returns one. Choose a name, such as <code>badge_cost</code>, that tells the reader what the function does.</p><div class=\"learning-block\"><p class=\"learning-label\">Worked walkthrough</p><h3>Trace a call back to its caller</h3><p>The caller is the code that runs the function. Follow cost = badge_cost(4, 3).</p><figure class=\"learning-visual\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Step</th><th scope=\"col\">What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Call</td><td>Supply arguments 4 and 3</td></tr><tr><td>Run the body</td><td>quantity is 4; unit_price is 3</td></tr><tr><td>Return</td><td>4 × 3 gives 12; the call ends</td></tr><tr><td>Assign</td><td>The caller stores 12 in cost</td></tr></tbody></table><figcaption>The result goes back to the call site. A later print(cost) displays it.</figcaption></figure><div class=\"learning-recall\"><p><strong>Pause and predict:</strong> If the body only prints 12 and reaches its end, what is stored in cost?</p><details class=\"print-answer-only\"><summary>Show the explanation</summary><p>None. Displaying 12 does not send the number back as the function result.</p></details><p><strong>Try a small change:</strong> Trace badge_cost(2, 5). Name the arguments, parameter values and returned value.</p></div></div>"],
['Names belong to scopes',"<p>A name created inside this function is <strong>local</strong> to it. This includes its parameters. A name created at module level, outside functions, is <strong>global</strong>. A local name and a global name can have the same spelling without being the same variable.</p><p>A function can read global names. However, using changing global values can make tests harder to understand. Pass the values the function needs as arguments and return its result where possible. Assigning to a local name does not automatically update a global name. <strong>Scope</strong> tells you where a name can be found and used.</p>"],
['Test the part before the whole',"<p>First test the calculation by itself. <code>badge_cost(0, 3)</code> should return 0, and <code>badge_cost(4, 3)</code> should return 12. Once these work, test the surrounding input and output. Testing one part at a time helps you find where a mistake occurs.</p><p>Keep the function focused on one task and indent its body consistently. Use comments to explain why you made a choice, rather than repeat what a line already says. A small, clearly named function is easier to test and change.</p><p><strong>Prepare for delivery_charge:</strong> use one parameter, <code>order_total</code>. “At least 50” means <code>order_total &gt;= 50</code>. Return the charge from each path. Once a path returns, that call has finished. Keep input prompts and printing in the calling code. Predict charges for a value below 50, exactly 50 and above 50 before testing.</p>"]
],code:'def badge_cost(quantity, unit_price):\n    return quantity * unit_price\n\nquantity = int(input("Number of badges: "))\ncost = badge_cost(quantity, 3)\nprint(f"Total: ${cost}")\n',practice:[['Identify','In def area(width, height) and area(5, 2), which are parameters and arguments?','width and height are parameters; 5 and 2 are arguments.'],['Explain','Why is return preferable to print for a reusable calculation?','The caller receives a result it can combine, test or display. Printing only produces output.'],['Predict','A function assigns x = 9 locally while global x is 2. Without a global declaration, what is global x after the call?','It remains 2. The local assignment binds a separate local name.']],task:"Write a function called delivery_charge. It must return 0 for an order total of at least 50, and 5 otherwise. Pass the total into the function rather than asking for input inside it. Test 49.99, 50 and 50.01.",solution:'def delivery_charge(order_total):\n    if order_total >= 50:\n        return 0\n    return 5\n\nfor total in [49.99, 50, 50.01]:\n    print(total, delivery_charge(total))\n# Expected charges: 5, 0, 0.\n',recap:"Give a function one clear task. Supply arguments, receive them through parameters, and return a result. Keep local names separate from global names.",lessons:['Model function definitions and calls.','Trace parameters, arguments and returns.','Compare local and global names; refactor a program.','Complete boundary tests for delivery_charge.'],reference:'https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/controlflow.html#defining-functions',refTitle:'Python function definitions',quiz:{q:'What does a function with no explicit return normally return?',options:['0','None','The last printed value'],answer:1,explanation:'A normal completed call without an explicit return value returns None.'}},
{id:'arrays',week:6,title:'Working with one-dimensional arrays',intro:"Store related values in a list. Visit them one at a time to find totals, minimums and maximums. These Python lists let you practice array-style algorithms.",objectives:['Load, access and display values using indices.','Calculate a total with traversal.','Find the index of a minimum or maximum and state assumptions.'],sections:[
['An array idea, a Python implementation',"<p>For <code>scores = [12, 7, 19]</code>, index 0 gives 12, index 1 gives 7 and index 2 gives 19. An <strong>index</strong> is an element’s position. You need one index to find an element in a one-dimensional array.</p><p>These tasks use Python lists to practice array algorithms. Lists can grow or shrink and can hold different types, unlike some fixed-size arrays. Use a consistent type for these tasks and follow the syllabus’s array conventions in pseudocode. The last valid non-negative index is <code>len(scores) - 1</code>. Here, accessing index 3 raises <code>IndexError</code>.</p>"],
['Load and traverse',"<p>Start with an empty list. Use <code>append</code> to add each accepted value. To display the list, visit its elements one at a time. This is called <strong>traversal</strong>. Use <code>range(len(scores))</code> if you also need each index. Remember: <code>scores[1]</code> is the value 7, while 1 is its index.</p><p>To add the values, set a running total to zero before the loop. Visit each value once and add it to the total. This running total is an <strong>accumulator</strong>. Python’s <code>sum</code> can do the job, but you should also be able to write and explain the traversal yourself.</p>"],
['Remember the location of the best value',"<p>The largest value is called the <strong>maximum</strong>. First check that the list is not empty. Then treat the first value as the largest and remember its index, 0, in <code>best_index</code>. Check the remaining values one at a time. Whenever you find a larger value, update <code>best_index</code> to its position.</p><p>Don’t start with a largest value of zero. For <code>[-8, -3]</code>, the largest value is <code>-3</code>. Starting with zero would give the wrong answer because zero is not in the list. This mistake is different from setting <code>best_index = 0</code>: that zero is a position, not the largest value.</p><p>If the largest value appears more than once, decide which position to keep:</p><ul><li>Use <code>&gt;</code> to keep the <strong>first</strong> occurrence. An equal value does not replace it.</li><li>Use <code>&gt;=</code> to keep the <strong>last</strong> occurrence. An equal value replaces the earlier one.</li></ul><p>For <code>[4, 9, 9]</code>, these return index 1 and index 2 respectively. This choice is your <strong>tie rule</strong>.</p><p>For the temperature task below, find the <strong>smallest</strong> value instead. Start at index 0 and change the comparison to <code>&lt;</code>. Keep the earlier index when values are equal. For <code>[-2, 4, -5, -5]</code>, the remembered index changes from 0 to 2, then stays at 2. This keeps the first <code>-5</code>.</p>"],
['Check edge cases',"<p>For <code>[12, 7, 19, 19]</code>, the total is 57. The first maximum is at index 2. Also test a one-element list, repeated equal values and negative values. An empty list has a total of zero but no maximum position. Check for it before reading index 0.</p><p>Python starts indexing at zero. A user may prefer “item 3” instead of “index 2”. Add 1 when displaying the position to that user. Keep the original zero-based index inside the algorithm.</p>"]
],code:'scores = [12, 7, 19, 19]\ntotal = 0\nfor score in scores:\n    total += score\nprint("Total:", total)\n\nif len(scores) == 0:\n    print("No maximum: the list is empty")\nelse:\n    best_index = 0\n    for index in range(1, len(scores)):\n        if scores[index] > scores[best_index]:\n            best_index = index\n    print("First maximum index:", best_index)\n    print("Maximum value:", scores[best_index])\n',practice:[['Predict','For [5, 9, 9], which index does the algorithm return when it compares values using >?','1, the first maximum. An equal value does not update the index.'],['Debug','Why can a maximum algorithm initialised to value 0 fail for [-8, -3]?','Zero is larger than every actual element. Start from a real element after checking the list is non-empty.'],['Trace','What is the final total for [4, -2, 6]?','8: accumulator states are 0, 4, 2 and 8.']],task:"Read four integer temperatures into a list. Display each index and its value. Find the index of the first smallest value without using min. Test [-2, 4, -5, -5] and explain which -5 is selected.",solution:'temperatures = []\nfor index in range(4):\n    temperatures.append(int(input("Temperature: ")))\nfor index in range(len(temperatures)):\n    print(index, temperatures[index])\nlowest_index = 0\nfor index in range(1, len(temperatures)):\n    if temperatures[index] < temperatures[lowest_index]:\n        lowest_index = index\nprint(lowest_index, temperatures[lowest_index])\n# Expected first minimum: index 2, value -5.\n',recap:"Visit each element in order. Keep its position separate from its value. Check for an empty list before finding a minimum or maximum, and decide what to do when that value appears more than once.",lessons:['Load and display an array-style list.','Trace and implement an accumulator.','Locate minimum/maximum positions.','Test edge cases and complete the temperature task.'],reference:'https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/datastructures.html',refTitle:'Python lists and data structures',quiz:{q:'For values = [8, 3, 6], what is values[1]?',options:['8','3','1'],answer:1,explanation:'Index 1 is the second element, whose value is 3.'}},
{id:'files',week:7,title:'Text files and reliable input',intro:'A program should preserve useful results and respond clearly when input is unsuitable. Combine file processing with deliberate validation.',objectives:['Read, write and append sequential text files.','Distinguish type checking from range checking.','Handle expected conversion errors and test invalid input.'],sections:[
['Accept only usable input',"<p>Check conversion and range separately. The text “-3” converts to an integer, but a negative badge quantity is not allowed. The text “three” cannot be converted to an integer at all. First try <code>int</code>; if it succeeds, check <code>quantity &gt;= 0</code>.</p><p>Catch the <code>ValueError</code> you expect from unsuitable text. Catching every possible error can hide bugs you need to fix. Tell the user what to enter and let them try again. These checks are <strong>validation</strong>. They can reject unsuitable values, but cannot prove a plausible value is truthful.</p><p>Read the worked program as a retry loop. <code>try</code> attempts the conversion. <code>except ValueError</code> handles failed conversion. <code>continue</code> skips the rest of this pass and asks again. <code>break</code> leaves the loop after an accepted value, so only accepted input reaches the file-writing code.</p>"],
['A file outlives the program',"<p>A program can read a saved file the next time it runs. This example stores one quantity on each line. Agree on the format before writing code: each line must contain one valid non-negative integer.</p><p>A <strong>path</strong> tells the program where to find a file. A relative name such as <code>quantities.txt</code> is resolved from the program’s current working directory. This may be different from the folder containing the script. Use a separate practice folder so the example cannot overwrite an important file.</p>"],
['Choose the mode deliberately',"<table><tr><th>Mode</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Important behaviour</th></tr><tr><td>r</td><td>Read</td><td>Missing file causes an error</td></tr><tr><td>w</td><td>Write</td><td>Existing contents are replaced</td></tr><tr><td>a</td><td>Append</td><td>New text goes at the end</td></tr></table><p>Choose the file mode before you open the file. Use <code>with</code> to make sure the file closes when the block ends, even if an error occurs. Set the encoding to UTF-8 so the text format is explicit.</p><p>Put a newline after each record. Without it, writing 2 and then 5 could produce the single quantity 25 instead of two records.</p>"],
['Read records in order',"<p>Read a sequential text file one line at a time. For each line:</p><ol><li>Remove surrounding whitespace with <code>strip</code>.</li><li>Convert the text to the required type.</li><li>Check any remaining rules, then use the value in your calculation.</li></ol><p>Decide what to do with a malformed line: reject the file, report its line number or skip it under a stated rule. Do not silently lose records.</p><p>Try valid input, zero, a negative value, letters and decimal text. Then test files with one record, several records and no records. Before a second run, predict what append mode will add.</p><p><strong>Worked reading example:</strong> create <code>quantities.txt</code> with 2, 0 and 5 on separate lines. Assume each line contains a valid non-negative integer. The code below reads those records and keeps a running total.</p><pre><code>total = 0\nwith open(\"quantities.txt\", \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\n    for line in file:\n        quantity = int(line.strip())\n        total += quantity\nprint(\"Total:\", total)</code></pre><div class=\"learning-block\"><p class=\"learning-label\">Worked walkthrough</p><h3>Turn each saved line into a value</h3><p>The line read from the file is text. strip() removes surrounding whitespace, including the line ending.</p><figure class=\"learning-visual\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Line read</th><th scope=\"col\">After strip()</th><th scope=\"col\">Integer</th><th scope=\"col\">Running total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>\"2\\n\"</td><td>\"2\"</td><td>2</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>\"0\\n\"</td><td>\"0\"</td><td>0</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>\"5\\n\"</td><td>\"5\"</td><td>5</td><td>7</td></tr></tbody></table><figcaption>The backslash-n notation represents a newline character. The file contains three separate records.</figcaption></figure><div class=\"learning-recall\"><p><strong>Pause and predict:</strong> What is printed for an existing file containing no records?</p><details class=\"print-answer-only\"><summary>Show the explanation</summary><p>Total: 0. The loop has no lines to process, so total keeps its starting value.</p></details><p><strong>Try a small change:</strong> Explain why an empty file, a blank line and a missing file are different cases.</p></div></div><p>An <strong>empty file</strong> has no records. A <strong>blank line</strong> becomes an empty string after <code>strip()</code>; <code>int(\"\")</code> fails. A <strong>missing file</strong> cannot be opened in read mode. The worked example assumes valid records and an existing file. Handling other cases is an extension: state the rule and report a problem rather than silently discarding data.</p>"]
],code:'while True:\n    try:\n        quantity = int(input("Non-negative quantity: "))\n    except ValueError:\n        print("Enter a whole number.")\n        continue\n    if quantity < 0:\n        print("Quantity cannot be negative.")\n        continue\n    break\n\nwith open("quantities.txt", "a", encoding="utf-8") as file:\n    file.write(f"{quantity}\\n")\nprint("Quantity saved.")\n',practice:[['Explain','Why does int("-4") not provide sufficient validation?','It succeeds as a type conversion, but -4 is outside the required non-negative range.'],['Predict','What happens if you use w instead of a on a second run?','The old contents are replaced when the file is opened, rather than retained and extended.'],['Test','Name a useful input that checks conversion failure.','Examples include three or 2.5. Neither is valid integer text for int conversion.']],task:"Make a practice file containing 2, 0 and 5, with one number per line. Read the lines in order and display their total. State what format each line must follow. Then test a file with no records.",solution:'total = 0\n# Assumption: every line contains a valid non-negative integer.\nwith open("quantities.txt", "r", encoding="utf-8") as file:\n    for line in file:\n        total += int(line.strip())\nprint("Total:", total)\n# Three supplied records -> 7. Empty file -> 0.\n# A missing file raises FileNotFoundError; malformed lines need a policy.\n',recap:"Check whether input can be converted, then check whether its value is allowed. Choose read, write or append deliberately. Use with to close files and agree on the record format.",lessons:['Model read/write/append and file paths.','Add range validation and specific exception handling.','Combine file processing with a test table.','Independent practice: adapt a fresh problem using weeks 1–7 skills.'],reference:'https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/inputoutput.html#reading-and-writing-files',refTitle:'Python file input and output',quiz:{q:'Which mode retains existing text and adds new records at the end?',options:['r','w','a'],answer:2,explanation:'Append mode adds text at the end without replacing the existing contents.'}}
];
